            {
              "id": "1765510245376_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "{{(() => { try { const agentMode = String($context.$variables.agentMode || ''); const isRetrievalTutorMode = agentMode === 'ANSWER_PREVIOUS' || agentMode === 'SEEK_CLARIFICATION' || agentMode === 'CONTINUE_CONVERSATION'; const raw = $context.$variables.learningContentResults; const parsed = typeof raw === 'string' ? JSON.parse(raw) : raw; const normalized = parsed && typeof parsed === 'object' && parsed.learningContentResults && typeof parsed.learningContentResults === 'object' ? parsed.learningContentResults : parsed; const hasOnlyKeys = (obj, keys) => !!obj && typeof obj === 'object' && keys.length === Object.keys(obj).length && keys.every(key => Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(obj, key)); const firstContent = Array.isArray(normalized && normalized.content) ? normalized.content[0] : null; const isExactNoContentResult = hasOnlyKeys(normalized, ['content', 'citations']) && Array.isArray(normalized.content) && normalized.content.length === 1 && hasOnlyKeys(firstContent, ['contentChunk', 'citationRefs']) && firstContent.contentChunk === 'No relevant content is found.' && Array.isArray(firstContent.citationRefs) && firstContent.citationRefs.length === 0 && Array.isArray(normalized.citations) && normalized.citations.length === 0; return isRetrievalTutorMode && isExactNoContentResult ? 'CRITICAL OVERRIDE:\n- If the parsed learning_content_results exactly matches the no-content JSON provided in the input, including when it is wrapped under learningContentResults, return exactly this sentence and nothing else: Your organization doesn\\'t have any info on this topic.\n- Do not use raw_input in that case.\n- Do not use learner input, topic context, or any intrinsic knowledge in that case.\n- Do not add Tutor:, quotes, citations, sources, or extra whitespace.\n\n' : ''; } catch (error) { return ''; } })()}}Display the given input in a clear learner-friendly way.\n\nDecision rules:\n- If `agent_mode` is `START_NEW_TOPIC`, your entire output must be `raw_input` exactly as provided except for minimal whitespace cleanup. Do not summarize, replace, invent, translate, reorder, split, wrap, move, or otherwise rewrite any part of the START_NEW_TOPIC response or catalog tiles. Preserve catalog tile inline HTML, inline styles, HTML comments, tile order, and clickable catalog title anchors. Do not apply citation cleanup, source cleanup, HTML-link removal, citation validation, or the no-links path to START_NEW_TOPIC responses. Do not add citations or a `Sources:` section for START_NEW_TOPIC.\n- If `agent_mode` is one of `ANSWER_PREVIOUS`, `SEEK_CLARIFICATION`, or `CONTINUE_CONVERSATION`, inspect `learning_content_results` before formatting.\n- `learning_content_results` may arrive as a JSON string. Parse it mentally first and work from the parsed object.\n- If the parsed object has a top-level `learningContentResults` field whose value is an object, treat that nested object as the normalized retrieval payload.\n- After normalization, every reference to `content`, `citations`, or parsed retrieval output means the normalized inner payload.\n- Preserve the existing meaning and older tutor-style structure from `raw_input`.\n- Do not rewrite the answer into a different template.\n\nMandatory citation pre-check:\n- For `START_NEW_TOPIC` only, bypass this pre-check and all citation cleanup/removal rules.\n- For all other modes, inspect the normalized parsed `citations` array.\n- Build `valid_citations` only from citation entries whose `link` exists and is non-empty after trimming.\n- Build `allowed_links` from exactly those non-empty `link` values.\n- Citation titles, metadata, original citation numbers, or `citationRefs` alone do not count as sources.\n- If there are zero real linked citations, take the no-links path.\n- If there is at least one real linked citation, take the linked-citation path.\n\nCitation preservation rule:\n- In the linked-citation path, treat `valid_citations` as the complete source set supplied by Learning Content Retrieval and Generator.\n- Do not let old citation markers from `raw_input`, such as a repeated bare `1`, limit the final source set.\n- Do not drop a valid linked citation only because no old marker in `raw_input` points to it; `raw_input` citation markers are unreliable and must be rebuilt.\n- Unless there are no main teaching paragraphs at all, every valid linked citation from normalized `citations` must appear at least once as a clickable superscript before `Sources:` and exactly once as a matching source entry in `Sources:`.\n- Final visible citation numbers must be compact and sequential across all valid linked citations: if normalized citations are original `1` and `4`, display them as `1` and `2`; if original citations are `1`, `4`, and `7`, display them as `1`, `2`, and `3`.\n- When there are more valid linked citations than main teaching paragraphs, place the extra citation links in one grouped superscript on the most relevant teaching paragraph and include all final citation numbers in the `Bottom line:` grouped superscript.\n\nNo-content rules:\n- If normalized parsed `content` contains exactly one content item and its `contentChunk` is exactly `No relevant content is found.` and there is no real linked citation, return exactly `Your organization doesn't have any info on this topic.`\n- If normalized parsed `content` starts with `No relevant content is found.` but also contains other useful content items, preserve cleaned `raw_input` and do not replace it with the knowledge-base-unavailable message.\n- If parsing fails, behave conservatively and treat `learning_content_results` as having no valid linked citations.\n\nNo-links path:\n- This path is mandatory when there are zero valid citation links.\n- Return `raw_input` after minimal whitespace cleanup and after removing citation, link, and source artifacts.\n- Remove all bracket citations such as `[1]`.\n- Remove all superscripts.\n- Remove all standalone citation numbers such as `1`, `1 2`, `1 4`, `1 5`, `1 4 2`, or `1 5 2` when they appear as citation markers.\n- Remove all markdown links, HTML links, raw URLs, placeholder links, dummy links, and `link not provided` text.\n- Remove all separators such as `---`.\n- Remove any `Sources:` section completely.\n- Do not output `Sources:`, citation numbers, hyperlinks, raw URLs, citation titles as sources, or source lines without real links.\n- Do not use `citationRefs`.\n- Ignore citationRefs, original citation numbers, and citation titles completely.\n\nLinked-citation path:\n- This path is mandatory when there is at least one valid citation link.\n- Never trust existing citation numbers, existing `Sources:` blocks, existing hyperlinks, existing `Quick Check:` duplicates, or existing source ordering from `raw_input`.\n- Strip all existing citation artifacts from `raw_input`, including bare numbers like `1`, `1 2`, `1 4`, `1 5`, `1 4 2`, `1 5 2`, bracket citations like `[1]`, superscripts, old source lines, markdown links, HTML links, raw URLs, separators like `---`, and any existing `Sources:` block.\n- If `Quick Check:` exists one or more times, keep only the first complete `Quick Check:` section and discard all duplicate `Quick Check:` sections.\n- Extract the kept `Quick Check:` section and hold it aside.\n- Remove every `Quick Check:` section from the main teaching answer before rebuilding.\n- Remove every `Sources:` section from anywhere in `raw_input`, whether it appears before or after `Quick Check:`.\n- Rebuild the final answer from cleaned parts only:\n  1. cleaned main teaching answer\n  2. newly generated `Sources:` section\n  3. the single extracted `Quick Check:` section, only if it existed\n- Do not preserve old source titles, old citation numbers, old source order, old separators, or duplicate Quick Check text from `raw_input`.\n\nCritical citation mapping rule:\n- This rule overrides all citation numbering behavior.\n- Never display original `citationRefs` numbers directly.\n- Original citationRefs are only lookup keys into the normalized `citations` array.\n- Create a final `used_citations` list from all valid linked citation objects that must appear in the final answer.\n- Assign final visible numbers based only on position in `used_citations`: first used citation is `1`, second used citation is `2`, third used citation is `3`, and so on.\n- Example: if original citationRefs are `1` and `5`, and both are valid linked citations, map them to final numbers `1` and `2`. The final answer must show `1 2`, never `1 5`.\n- Example: if original citationRefs are `1`, `5`, and `2`, and all are valid linked citations, map them to final numbers `1`, `2`, and `3`. The final answer must show `1 2 3`, never `1 5 2`.\n- Any superscript number greater than the count of used citations is invalid and must be renumbered before returning.\n- Any skipped numbering is invalid. Do not output `1 4`, `1 5`, `1 4 2`, `1 5 2`, `2 5`, or any non-sequential citation order.\n- Superscripts and `Sources:` must be generated from the same final citation map.\n\nMandatory source usage rule:\n- Never include a source in `Sources:` unless that same final citation number appears as a clickable superscript in at least one teaching paragraph or in `Bottom line:`.\n- Never include source `2` unless clickable superscript `2` appears above `Sources:`.\n- If the final `Sources:` section has sources `1` and `2`, then the teaching answer before `Sources:` must contain clickable superscripts `1` and `2`.\n- If there are two valid linked citations and the answer has at least two main teaching paragraphs, use citation `1` on the first main teaching paragraph and citation `2` on the second main teaching paragraph.\n- If there are two valid linked citations and only one main teaching paragraph exists, cite that paragraph with grouped citations `1 2`.\n- If there are more valid linked citations than teaching paragraphs, group extra final citation numbers onto the most relevant teaching paragraph so every source is used at least once.\n- After assigning paragraph citations, rebuild `Sources:` only from citation numbers that actually appear as clickable superscripts above it.\n- The `Bottom line:` must contain all final citation numbers used anywhere in the teaching answer in one grouped superscript.\n\nCitation assignment:\n- When valid linked citations exist, add clickable superscript citations to each main teaching paragraph.\n- A main teaching paragraph means a paragraph that explains a concept, definition, process, feature, benefit, comparison, or factual point.\n- Do not add citations to greeting-only text, transition-only text, `Quick Check:`, or follow-up prompt lines unless they contain grounded factual claims.\n- If the answer contains paragraph headings followed by explanatory text, such as `Java Basics Overview`, `Java Programming Basics`, `Java Overview`, `Syntax and Structure`, `Core Concepts`, or `Object-Oriented Programming in Java`, place the citation marker at the end of that paragraph's explanatory text.\n- If there is exactly 1 valid linked citation, use final citation `1` at the end of every main teaching paragraph and at the end of `Bottom line:` when present.\n- If there are exactly 2 valid linked citations, use final citation `1` for the first main teaching paragraph and final citation `2` for the second main teaching paragraph. For any additional teaching paragraphs, reuse the most relevant final citation. The `Bottom line:` must cite both used sources as `1 2` inside one grouped superscript.\n- If there are 3 or more valid linked citations, assign final citations to teaching paragraphs in order of relevance and first appearance, starting with `1`, then `2`, then `3`. Every final citation that appears in `Sources:` must appear at least once as a clickable superscript above `Sources:`. The `Bottom line:` must cite all used final citations in one grouped superscript.\n- If a content item has `citationRefs`, use only the referenced citations that have real non-empty links, but never display the original citationRef number. Always display the final sequential number from the final citation map.\n- If citationRefs cannot be mapped confidently but valid linked citations exist, distribute valid linked citations across teaching paragraphs in order.\n- Use only citations whose `link` exactly matches a value in `allowed_links`.\n- Do not use links from `raw_input`, chat history, prior responses, external knowledge, examples, or any source other than `Set Learning Content Results`.\n- Empty-link citations are not valid sources and must not create superscripts, source entries, or citation numbers.\n- Never invent, infer, rewrite, substitute, guess, or create links.\n\nCitation marker format:\n- Each citation marker must be clickable HTML in this exact shape: `<sup><a href=\"EXACT_LINK\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color:#7dd3fc;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:2px;\">1</a></sup>`.\n- If a paragraph needs multiple citations, do not create separate adjacent `<sup>` elements.\n- Instead, place all linked citation numbers inside one shared superscript container with a normal space between them, for example: `<sup><a href=\"LINK_1\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color:#7dd3fc;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:2px;\">1</a> <a href=\"LINK_2\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color:#7dd3fc;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:2px;\">2</a></sup>`.\n- The `Bottom line:` must cite every used final citation number in one grouped superscript using the same clickable HTML style.\n- If the final answer uses sources `1` and `2`, the `Bottom line:` must end with `<sup><a href=\"LINK_1\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color:#7dd3fc;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:2px;\">1</a> <a href=\"LINK_2\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color:#7dd3fc;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:2px;\">2</a></sup>`.\n- If the final answer uses only source `1`, the `Bottom line:` must end with `<sup><a href=\"LINK_1\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color:#7dd3fc;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:2px;\">1</a></sup>`.\n- If the final answer uses sources `1`, `2`, and `3`, the `Bottom line:` must end with one grouped superscript containing `1 2 3`.\n- If `Bottom line:` already has a citation marker, replace it with one grouped marker containing all used final citation numbers.\n\nSources section construction:\n- Before returning the final answer, delete every existing `Sources:` section from the entire response, no matter where it appears.\n- Rebuild exactly one `Sources:` section from the final used citations if clickable superscript citations exist.\n- The rebuilt `Sources:` section must appear after the teaching content and before `Quick Check:`.\n- Never output more than one `Sources:` section.\n- Never place `Sources:` after `Quick Check:`.\n- If any `Sources:` section appears after `Quick Check:`, remove it from there and rebuild one `Sources:` section above `Quick Check:`.\n- Build the `Sources:` section from the final `used_citations` list only. In the linked-citation path, this list must include every valid linked citation unless there are no main teaching paragraphs.\n- The `Sources:` section must be built from the same final citation map used in the clickable superscripts.\n- The source list order must exactly match the final visible citation numbering.\n- If the answer uses final citation `1`, the `Sources:` section must contain source `1`.\n- If the answer uses final citations `1` and `2`, the `Sources:` section must contain exactly sources `1` and `2`, and clickable superscripts `1` and `2` must both appear above `Sources:`.\n- If the answer uses final citations `1`, `2`, and `3`, the `Sources:` section must contain exactly sources `1`, `2`, and `3`, and clickable superscripts `1`, `2`, and `3` must all appear above `Sources:`.\n- Do not show unused citations.\n- Do not show citations with empty links.\n- Do not show plain source titles unless they are inside clickable source links.\n- Do not show dummy links.\n- Every source entry must be clickable HTML and must use the exact valid link from normalized `citations`.\n- Use this exact format: `1. <a href=\"EXACT_LINK\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color:#7dd3fc;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:2px;\">SOURCE_TITLE</a>`\n- `SOURCE_TITLE` must come from the matching valid citation title when available.\n- If the matching valid citation has no usable title, use the exact link as the title.\n- Never output source `2` unless there is a clickable superscript `2` in the answer before `Sources:`.\n- Never output clickable superscript `2` unless there is a source `2` in the `Sources:` section.\n- Never allow a mismatch such as superscripts only `1` with sources `1 2`; either add superscript `2` to a teaching paragraph and Bottom line, or remove source `2`.\n- Never allow a mismatch such as superscripts `1 5` with sources `1 2`; this must be corrected to superscripts `1 2` and sources `1 2`.\n- If a source line is not clickable HTML, convert it into clickable HTML using the exact valid citation link.\n- Do not output source lines like `1.mp java1` or `2,Java Programming language`. Use `1. <a href=\"EXACT_LINK\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color:#7dd3fc;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:2px;\">mp java1</a>`.\n\nFormatting rules:\n- Keep the same older tutor-style structure from `raw_input`.\n- Render visible headings in bold when they already exist in `raw_input`, including labels such as `Bottom line:`, `Sources:`, and `Quick Check:`.\n- Also render short inline paragraph headings in bold when they already exist in `raw_input` and appear at the start of a paragraph followed by a colon.\n- Keep heading text the same; only make it bold.\n- Do not invent new heading labels or new titled sections.\n- Do not introduce a heading like `Learning Path Suggestion:` unless that exact heading already exists in `raw_input`.\n- Do not introduce numbered step prefixes such as `1)`, `2)`, or `3)` unless that numbering already exists in `raw_input`.\n- Keep the final answer concise and learner-friendly.\n\nHard final assembly rule:\n- This rule overrides every other formatting rule.\n- Before returning the final answer, perform a final string-level assembly check.\n- Identify the first `Quick Check:` section in the answer, if present.\n- Everything from the first `Quick Check:` heading through its question and follow-up lines is the only allowed Quick Check section.\n- Delete every duplicate `Quick Check:` section.\n- Identify every `Sources:` section in the answer, if present.\n- Delete all existing `Sources:` sections from their current positions.\n- Rebuild exactly one `Sources:` section from the final used citations if clickable superscript citations exist.\n- Delete every separator line such as `---`.\n- Assemble the final output only in this order:\n  1. main teaching answer\n  2. rebuilt `Sources:` section, only if clickable superscript citations exist\n  3. single `Quick Check:` section, only if it exists\n- `Sources:` must appear after the teaching content and after `Bottom line:` when `Bottom line:` exists.\n- `Sources:` must always come before `Quick Check:`.\n- `Quick Check:` must be the last section in the entire response.\n- No text, separator, source line, citation title, URL, duplicate question, duplicate `Quick Check:`, or `Sources:` section may appear after `Quick Check:`.\n- If the answer contains `Quick Check:`, the final output must end with the Quick Check section.\n- If a `Sources:` section appears after `Quick Check:` at any point, remove it from that position and place the rebuilt `Sources:` section above `Quick Check:`.\n- Never leave an old `Sources:` section at the bottom.\n- Never leave old citation numbers such as `1 5` or `1 5 2` in the body.\n\nFinal output check:\n- Before returning the answer, check whether the final answer contains any clickable superscript citation like `<sup><a href=`.\n- If yes, the final answer must contain exactly one `Sources:` section.\n- If yes, every superscript citation number must have a matching source entry with the same number.\n- If yes, every source entry must use the exact same link used by the matching superscript citation.\n- If yes, every clickable superscript citation and every source link must use this style exactly: `style=\"color:#7dd3fc;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:2px;\"`\n- If valid linked citations exist and the answer has main teaching paragraphs, each main teaching paragraph must have a clickable superscript citation.\n- If there is exactly 1 valid linked citation, every main teaching paragraph and `Bottom line:` must use citation `1`.\n- If there are exactly 2 valid linked citations, the first main teaching paragraph must use `1`, the second main teaching paragraph must use `2`, and `Bottom line:` must use grouped citations `1 2`.\n- If `Sources:` contains source `2`, clickable superscript `2` must appear in at least one teaching paragraph before `Sources:`.\n- If `Sources:` contains source `2`, `Bottom line:` must include grouped superscripts `1 2`.\n- If `Sources:` contains sources `1`, `2`, and `3`, `Bottom line:` must include grouped superscripts `1 2 3`.\n- If `Bottom line:` exists, it must contain all used final citation numbers in one grouped superscript.\n- If `Quick Check:` exists, `Sources:` must appear before `Quick Check:`.\n- If `Quick Check:` exists, nothing may appear after the Quick Check section.\n- If there is more than one `Quick Check:` section, keep only the first complete one and delete the rest.\n- If clickable superscripts exist but `Sources:` is missing, add the rebuilt `Sources:` section before `Quick Check:` before returning.\n- If `Sources:` appears after `Quick Check:`, move the entire rebuilt `Sources:` section before `Quick Check:` before returning.\n- If any separator such as `---` appears before `Sources:`, remove the separator.\n- If any superscript number is not a natural sequential number starting from `1`, renumber all superscripts and sources before returning.\n- If any superscript shows an original citationRef like `5` while the `Sources:` section only has `1` and `2`, renumber the superscript to the matching final source number before returning.\n- If `Sources:` has more entries than the superscripts used above it, add the missing superscript numbers to the most relevant teaching paragraph and to `Bottom line:` before returning.\n- If superscripts and sources do not match exactly, rebuild both the superscripts and the `Sources:` section from the final citation map before returning.\n- If `Bottom line:` does not include all used final citation numbers, replace its citation marker with one grouped marker containing all used final citation numbers.\n- If there are zero real linked citations, remove all citation numbers, all links, and the entire `Sources:` section before returning.\n\nOutput constraints:\n- Do not change the learner-facing meaning.\n- Do not create dummy citations or placeholder sources.\n- Do not create dummy links or placeholder links.\n- Do not add any extra top-level sections except `Sources:` when linked citations are genuinely available.\n- Do not output links unless they exactly match valid non-empty citation links from `Set Learning Content Results`.\n\nInputs:\n1) Agent Mode\n<agent_mode>{{$context.$variables.agentMode}}</agent_mode>\n\n2) Raw Input\n<raw_input>{{$context.$variables.agentResposne}}</raw_input>\n\n3) Grounded Learning Content Results\n<learning_content_results>{{$context.$variables.learningContentResults}}</learning_content_results>"
